# Troubleshooting

## The tablet does not connect over USB

- Use a cable that carries data. Charge-only cables power the tablet but never show up to the Mac.
- Start the Mac app first, then plug in. If Android asked "Open Ligarview?" and you dismissed it, unplug and replug.
- Do not run the host with `--adb` at the same time: it replaces the USB transport.

## The tablet does not appear in the Mac window, or Wi-Fi will not connect

- Open the Android app first, on its **Wi-Fi** tab, and unplug the USB cable.
- Both devices must be on the same network. Routers with client isolation, guest networks and some mesh systems block the Bonjour discovery the pairing relies on.
- If the tablet shows "refused", the code is wrong or sharing is off on the Mac. Press **Unpair** and pair again with the code currently on the tablet.

## The pen, fingers or keyboard do nothing

- Turn on the matching "Allow … from the tablet" checkbox in the Ligarview window. They are off by default, and a sidebar button that the Mac does not allow is dimmed.
- Allow **Ligarview** in **System Settings → Privacy & Security → Accessibility**. Without it nothing reaches the Mac, and the window says so. If it is not in the list, turn a checkbox off and on again so macOS asks.
- Input only works while the picture is full screen.
- After rebuilding the app from source, macOS may need the permission again; use `tools/bundle.sh` so the signature keeps its identifier.

## Zoom does nothing, or scrolling zooms

Pinching sends Ctrl and scroll, which Chromium-based browsers, Figma, Excalidraw and many map apps read as zoom. Safari and Preview only understand the trackpad's own pinch and ignore it. If scrolling ever zooms, a Ctrl is stuck: leave full screen and come back, or turn the pen checkbox off and on.

## A modifier key seems stuck

Tap its sidebar button again to let go. Leaving full screen, switching the pen checkbox off and unplugging the cable also release everything the tablet was holding.

## The Dock button does nothing

It pushes the pointer against the bottom edge of the tablet's display, which brings up an auto-hidden Dock. Turn on **Automatically hide and show the Dock** in System Settings, and make sure the Dock is at the bottom of the screen.

## macOS keeps asking for Screen Recording

Remove the old **Ligarview** entry in **Privacy & Security → Screen & System Audio Recording**, add it again, and reopen the app. If you build from source, use `tools/bundle.sh`: it signs the app with a requirement tied to its identifier so the permission survives rebuilds.

## The picture is blurry

The display must reach its HiDPI mode (2560x1600 real pixels for 1280x800 points). The app reports the mode it selected; a different `--scale` or a size macOS refuses will fall back to a blurrier one.

## It feels laggy

- Turn off **Power saving** on the tablet and set **Motion smoothness** to Adaptive. The info screen tells you when either is off.
- Prefer USB; on Wi-Fi use 5 GHz and stay close to the router.
- On a 120 Hz tablet try `--fps 120 --bitrate 60`.
- Try `--low-latency-rc` for about 10 ms less encode latency, at the cost of choppier window dragging.

## The cursor is missing

The cursor travels on its own connection and is drawn by the tablet. If it vanishes, move the mouse once; the host resends the shape whenever it changes. If it stays missing, restart the app and report it on GitHub with the host log.

## You should not have to restart anything

| Situation | What happens |
|---|---|
| USB cable unplugged and replugged | The Mac switches the tablet to accessory mode again and the app reconnects |
| Mac app restarted | The host asks the tablet app whether it wants a stream, and the app restarts its session |
| Wi-Fi drops or the host freezes | The host sends a heartbeat every second; 4 s of silence counts as a dead link and the app reconnects |
| Tablet sleeps or locks | The app reconnects when it returns to the foreground |
| Mac sleeps | The host recreates the display, HiDPI mode, capture and encoder, then asks for a keyframe |
